  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Sig Sauer has recorded $316M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 303 awards with an average award size of $1M. Obligations fell 22.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a run-up from $17.2M in FY21 to $407M in FY25, followed by $316M in FY26. The Department of Defense accounted for the bulk of buying activity with $308M in obligations. Smaller buyers include the Department of Homeland Security with $6.1M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $737K. Sig Sauer’s FY2026 market footprint centers on Small Arms, Ordnance, and Ordnance Accessories Manufacturing at $306M and Small Arms Ammunition Manufacturing at $9.7M. Other listed NAICS categories show minimal or zero obligations. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date), and the page reports obligations rather than total contract ceilings for most entries. This snapshot highlights concentration in a single agency and in a narrow set of manufacturing markets, with FY2026 obligations below the FY25 total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What is Sig Sauer's FY2026 federal contracting snapshot?",
        "body": "Sig Sauer obligated $316M in FY2026 to date across 303 awards, with an average award size of $1M. Obligations fell 22.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show $17.2M in FY21, $92.9M in FY22, $135M in FY23, $211M in FY24, $407M in FY25, and $316M in FY26. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date)."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Sig Sauer's main government customers and what markets do they buy?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $308M for Sig Sauer in FY2026 to date. Other buyers include the Department of Homeland Security with $6.1M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $737K. The largest NAICS market is Small Arms, Ordnance, and Ordnance Accessories Manufacturing at $306M, followed by Small Arms Ammunition Manufacturing at $9.7M."
      }
    ]
  },
